On a curve, the polar curvature separates the singularity data of a model from the area of the background. A smooth gauge change reduces any background of positive area to a positive form. Keeping the polar measure fixed preserves distances whenever both backgrounds admit it, while rescaling by the ratio of the areas identifies the entire model spaces; the gauge function controls the normalized representatives. At zero area the whole model space collapses to the unique normalized potential. We keep the curve $Y$ of section 13.28 , and we fix a smooth positive $(1,1)$-form $\eta $ on $Y$ with $\int _ Y\eta =1$, which we use for $L^1$ norms and capacities. In this section any smooth real $(1,1)$-form $\sigma $ on $Y$ with $A_\sigma =\int _ Y\sigma \geq 0$ is allowed as a background. By lemma 5.29.22 , these are exactly the forms for which $\mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$ is nonempty, and $\sigma =A_\sigma \eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$ for a smooth real function $h$. Let $\mathcal M(\sigma )$ be the set of ordinary models in $\mathrm{PSH}(Y,\sigma )$, and put $U_\sigma (p)=\int _ Y\mathrm{MA}_\sigma (p)$. The volume comparison $d_\sigma $ is defined by the formula of definition 13.22.1 ; at zero area it vanishes identically. Polar curvatures, their atomic parts and ideal models are defined as before, with $\sigma $ in place of $	heta $. Proposition 13.29.1 . C9C98321BEFC Polar curvature identifies $\mathcal M(\sigma )$ with the set of polar measures of mass at most $A_\sigma $. Write $p_\sigma (\mu )$ for the model with polar measure $\mu $. Then \[  U_\sigma (p_\sigma (\mu ))=A_\sigma -\mu (Y),\qquad d_\sigma (p_\sigma (\mu ),p_\sigma (
