u \| _{\mathrm{TV}},  \] and ideal projection corresponds to taking the atomic part. The space $(\mathcal M(\sigma ),d_\sigma )$ is complete and geodesic, and convergence for $d_\sigma $ implies convergence in $L^1$ and in capacity. If $A_\sigma =0$, its only member is the smooth potential $u_\sigma $ of lemma 5.29.22 . Proof. Suppose first that $A=A_\sigma >0$, and write $\sigma =A\eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$. For $v\in \mathrm{PSH}(Y,A\eta )$, the inequalities for ordinary envelopes in proposition 18.5.1 give \[  P_{A\eta }[v]+\min _ Yh \leq P_\sigma [v-h]+h \leq P_{A\eta }[v]+\max _ Yh.  \] For a model $p$ in background $A\eta $, the potential $q=P_\sigma [p-h]$ therefore satisfies $q+h\sim p$, and since the envelope depends only on the ordinary type, $P_\sigma [q]=P_\sigma [p-h]=q$. Conversely, for $q\in \mathcal M(\sigma )$, the same bounds with $v=q+h$ show that $P_{A\eta }[q+h]\sim q+h$, and taking the envelope back recovers $q$. Thus $p\mapsto P_\sigma [p-h]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal M(A\eta )$ onto $\mathcal M(\sigma )$. Smooth gauge changes preserve curvature currents, and ordinary equivalence preserves their polar parts by corollary 5.27.15 . Consequently the bijection preserves polar curvature. It also preserves singularity volumes, and subtracting the same smooth function commutes with maxima. The classification and the distance formula of lemma 13.28.1 , theorem 13.28.4 , applied to the positive form $A\eta $, now give the first assertions. The same gauge bounds for ideal envelopes show that the transported ideal projections are ordinarily equivalent. Both are ordinary models by corollary 18.4.4 , and ordinary models of the same type are equal. Thus the bijection commutes with ideal projection, which still takes the atomic part. Completeness and the geodesics follow from the identification with polar measures, as in corollary 13.28.7 . For convergence to a model of positive mass, note that $\sigma $ is big because $\sigma +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm